zwanth@sunflower.com (Zwanth) writes:
> On nethack.alt.org, I had gotten the Amulet of Yendor and was making
> my way back up through the dungeon.  I ate some food item and these
> messages appeared:
> 
> Blecch!  Rotten food!  The world spins and goes dark.  --More--
> A voice booms out... --More--
> You are conscious again.
> 
> The Wizard was indeed resurrected, but what happened to the "So thou
> thought thou couldst kill me, fool." message?  If I was unconscious,
> how could I hear the voice booming out in the first place?  Shouldn't
> there be an ifcheck here for if one is unconscious?

This bug is caused by the use of two different functions to give the
two halves of the message:

        pline("A voice booms out...");
        verbalize("So thou thought thou couldst %s me, fool.", verb);

verbalize() has a check for flags.soundok, pline() doesn't.
flags.soundok is set false by the fainting code, and reset when you
regain consciousness.

